<p>🏅 <strong>Approval testing</strong> is a tool that helps us put a piece of <strong>untested legacy code</strong> under a test harness in a speedy way, so that we can start refactoring it. We generate a text output from our code, usually called the &quot;<strong>Golden Master</strong>&quot;, using an explosion of <strong>inputs</strong> and data combinations, and compare the text <strong>output</strong> produced by the tests with that Golden Master. If we change the behavior while refactoring, the output of our tests would change, and a diff with the initial snapshot would be generated.</p>

<blockquote>
&quot;Golden Master&quot; is not a great name for the snapshot, as it implies that it will never change, and this is not always true. But everybody will understand what you mean! Other than that, you can use the term “Approval testing” to refer to the technique.
</blockquote>


<h2>Basics</h2>
<p>We start by identifying the <strong>inputs and outputs</strong> of the system, then writing some approval tests based on that. Approval tests have a different way to write assertions. Instead of:</p>

<pre><code class="language-python">assert(a == b)</code></pre>
<p>we do:</p>
<pre><code class="language-python">verify(a)</code></pre>

<p>If no Golden Master exists when we run these tests, a snapshot of the current test results will be created, you can then &quot;<strong>approve it</strong>&quot; and use it as your Golden Master. Whenever our current results differ from it, we can examine the differences. Either we will <strong>update the master</strong> because we expected the changes and they are good, or we will go back to our code and update or <strong>roll back our changes</strong> to get our results back in line with the Golden Master.</p>
<blockquote>
The recommendation is to start with a Golden Master for the piece of code that is not covered by tests, and then use TDD to write proper unit tests as we refactor it.
</blockquote>

<p>Once we are done refactoring and have the suite of tests that we needed, it&#x27;s up to us to keep the approval tests or not. I don&#x27;t think they contribute much after we have the other tests in place, and they introduce extra maintenance load. What&#x27;s more, they are not super descriptive so they are bad at documenting behavior (which is one of the things we want our tests to do). Keeping them or not is more a matter of how paranoid we are and if the paranoia is big enough to justify the maintenance.</p>

<p>In any case, <strong>they are no substitute for the other types of tests</strong>.</p>

<h2>Code example, step by step</h2>
<blockquote>
I recommend you follow the steps below in your local machine as you read, to get a hang of how it works.
</blockquote>

<p>Let&#x27;s say we have this bit of ugly legacy code with no tests at all, and we want to put it in a test harness so that we can start refactoring it safely:</p>

<pre><code class="language-python">
# gilded_rose.py

class Item:
    def __init__(self, name, sell_in, quality):
        self.name = name
        self.sell_in = sell_in
        self.quality = quality

class GildedRose(object):
    def __init__(self, items):
        self.items = items

    def update_quality(self):
        for item in self.items:
            if item.name != &quot;Aged Brie&quot; and item.name != &quot;Backstage passes to a TAFKAL80ETC concert&quot;:
                if item.quality &gt; 0:
                    if item.name != &quot;Sulfuras, Hand of Ragnaros&quot;:
                        item.quality = item.quality - 1
            else:
                if item.quality &lt; 50:
                    item.quality = item.quality + 1
                    if item.name == &quot;Backstage passes to a TAFKAL80ETC concert&quot;:
                        if item.sell_in &lt; 11:
                            if item.quality &lt; 50:
                                item.quality = item.quality + 1
                        if item.sell_in &lt; 6:
                            if item.quality &lt; 50:
                                item.quality = item.quality + 1
            if item.name != &quot;Sulfuras, Hand of Ragnaros&quot;:
                item.sell_in = item.sell_in - 1
            if item.sell_in &lt; 0:
                if item.name != &quot;Aged Brie&quot;:
                    if item.name != &quot;Backstage passes to a TAFKAL80ETC concert&quot;:
                        if item.quality &gt; 0:
                            if item.name != &quot;Sulfuras, Hand of Ragnaros&quot;:
                                item.quality = item.quality - 1
                    else:
                        item.quality = item.quality - item.quality
                else:
                    if item.quality &lt; 50:
                        item.quality = item.quality + 1
</code></pre>

<p>This is a great candidate for using approval testing. We need to create a Golden Master, so we identify that the <strong>input</strong> is the items that we pass in the constructor, and we don&#x27;t have any <strong>outputs</strong> at the moment but we can start asserting on the fields of the items.</p>

<p>We will start with a simple approval test. We could manage this manually, but it is more comfortable to use <a href="https://github.com/approvals/ApprovalTests.Python">the <code class="language-python">approvaltests</code> library</a> (also check <a href="https://github.com/approvals/Approvals.NodeJS">the JavaScript version</a>) which will handle everything for us:</p>

<pre><code class="language-bash">$ pip install approvaltests</code></pre>

<p>Let&#x27;s write a simple approval test for the <code class="language-python">update_qualty</code> method of the class <code class="language-python">GildedRose</code>. A good name for our test class, following the convention <strong>TestClassNameMethodName</strong> is <code class="language-python">TestGildedRoseUpdateQuality</code>:</p>

<pre><code class="language-python">
import unittest

from approvaltests.approvals import verify

from app.gilded_rose import Item, GildedRose


class TestGildedRoseUpdateQuality(unittest.TestCase):
    def test_updates_quality(self):
        items = [Item(&quot;name&quot;, 0, 0)]
        gilded_rose = GildedRose(items)
        gilded_rose.update_quality()
        verify(gilded_rose.items[0].name)

if __name__ == &#x27;__main__&#x27;:
    unittest.main()</code></pre>

<p>We can run this test and it will fail:</p>

<pre><code class="language-bash">
$ python3 -m unittest discover -v
test_updates_quality (tests.test_gilded_rose.TestGildedRoseUpdateQuality) ... ERROR
======================================================================
ERROR: test_updates_quality (tests.test_gilded_rose.TestGildedRoseUpdateQuality)
----------------------------------------------------------------------
Traceback (most recent call last):
approvaltests.approval_exception.ApprovalException: Approval Mismatch, received != approved
	Approved: /path/to/gilded_rose/tests/TestGildedRoseUpdateQuality.test_updates_quality.approved.txt
	Received: /path/to/gilded_rose/tests/TestGildedRoseUpdateQuality.test_updates_quality.received.txt</code></pre>

<p>It fails because we didn&#x27;t have an &quot;approved&quot; file, however it already created both an &quot;approved&quot; and a &quot;received&quot; file for us:</p>

<pre><code class="language-plaintext">
$ tree tests
tests
├── TestGildedRoseUpdateQuality.test_updates_quality.approved.txt
├── TestGildedRoseUpdateQuality.test_updates_quality.received.txt
├── __init__.py
└── test_guilded_rose.py</code></pre>

<p>The &quot;approved&quot; file is empty, because we didn&#x27;t have a file, the &quot;received&quot; one contains the output:</p>

<pre><code class="language-bash">
$ TestGildedRoseUpdateQuality.test_updates_quality.received.txt
name</code></pre>

<p>That is the output of <code class="language-python">gilded_rose.items[0].name</code>.</p>
<p>If we rename that file so that instead of &quot;received&quot; it says &quot;approved&quot;:</p>

<pre><code class="language-plaintext">
$ mv tests/TestGildedRoseUpdateQuality.test_updates_quality.received.txt tests/TestGildedRoseUpdateQuality.test_updates_quality.approved.txt

$ tree tests
tests
├── TestGildedRoseUpdateQuality.test_updates_quality.approved.txt
├── __init__.py
└── test_guilded_rose.py</code></pre>

<p>And run the tests again:</p>

<pre><code class="language-bash">
$ python3 -m unittest discover -v
test_updates_quality (tests.test_guilded_rose.TestGildedRoseUpdateQuality) ... ok

----------------------------------------------------------------------
Ran 1 test in 0.009s

OK</code></pre>

<p>They pass.</p>

<p>If the difference opens in our code editor, we can also just copy-paste the value on the left to the file on the right (or click the little arrow if you get one) and save. The library will remove the &quot;received&quot; file automatically the next time we run the tests:</p>

